    "summary": "Verified motion prompt by @alex_prompter",
    "contentBody": "Adopt the role of an expert motion designer. Build a 30-second animated explainer for my business as a single HTML page. 5 scenes. The customer's problem, what I do, how it works in 3 steps, one proof point, and my name at the end. Bold text, smooth transitions, my brand colours. My business [DESCRIBE WHAT YOU SELL, WHO IT'S FOR AND YOUR COLOURS]",
    "originalPrompt": "Adopt the role of an expert motion designer. Build a 30-second animated explainer for my business as a single HTML page. 5 scenes. The customer's problem, what I do, how it works in 3 steps, one proof point, and my name at the end. Bold text, smooth transitions, my brand colours. My business [DESCRIBE WHAT YOU SELL, WHO IT'S FOR AND YOUR COLOURS]",
    "improvedPrompt": "<direction>\nA {{length}}-second animated explainer for my business, as a single HTML page: {{business}}.\nFive scenes: the customer's problem, what I do, how it works in three steps, one proof point, and my name at the end.\nBold type, smooth transitions where each scene is made out of the last, my brand colors: {{colors}}.\nBanned: particle bursts, glows, lens flares, camera shake, RGB split, crossfades, generic gradients, bouncy easing, holds longer than half a second, anything that looks like a template.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM. Equal time for each scene, with the three steps getting a beat each.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a kick on every beat and a hit on each scene change. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<start>\nShow me the copy for each scene and a storyboard, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the HTML page and the MP4.\n</start>",
    "template": "<direction>\nA {{length}}-second animated explainer for my business, as a single HTML page: {{business}}.\nFive scenes: the customer's problem, what I do, how it works in three steps, one proof point, and my name at the end.\nBold type, smooth transitions where each scene is made out of the last, my brand colors: {{colors}}.\nBanned: particle bursts, glows, lens flares, camera shake, RGB split, crossfades, generic gradients, bouncy easing, holds longer than half a second, anything that looks like a template.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM. Equal time for each scene, with the three steps getting a beat each.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a kick on every beat and a hit on each scene change. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<start>\nShow me the copy for each scene and a storyboard, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the HTML page and the MP4.\n</start>",
