    "summary": "Verified motion prompt by @HO_BA",
    "contentBody": "Make a dynamic 30-second motion graphics video that shows what an incredible motion designer you are, like it's your showreel for a résumé. go all out.  Make it a video to introduce https://t.co/xdCXEdKZRq Use actual product screenshot/logo/assets Must have music and motion must match the music  Do it like a real professional production video, not like a demo or prototype. Add more animation and motion design; avoid using screenshots as raw; instead, break them down into components/icons so we can animate those too. Make the motions more juicy. Use the best motion design techniques you can.",
    "originalPrompt": "Make a dynamic 30-second motion graphics video that shows what an incredible motion designer you are, like it's your showreel for a résumé. go all out.  Make it a video to introduce https://t.co/xdCXEdKZRq Use actual product screenshot/logo/assets Must have music and motion must match the music  Do it like a real professional production video, not like a demo or prototype. Add more animation and motion design; avoid using screenshots as raw; instead, break them down into components/icons so we can animate those too. Make the motions more juicy. Use the best motion design techniques you can.",
    "improvedPrompt": "<inputs>\nAsk me for anything below I haven't given you. If I skip it, use the default: 3 to 6 screenshots of the real interface (default: capture them yourself), the logo (default: take it from the site), brand colors and font (default: {{brand}}), and a royalty-free track with a clear beat (default: compose one in code).\n</inputs>\n\n<direction>\nA {{length}}-second introduction video for {{product}}. Use the product screenshots and assets I attach. Never invent screens, features or numbers.\nPick one through-line object from the product itself (its cursor, a key button, the logo's mark, a single data point) and make every scene a transformation of it: it starts in the UI, changes shape or role in each scene, and becomes the logo on the end card. Name it before you storyboard.\nRebuild the real UI as vector layers so its parts can move on their own; never slide a flat screenshot in. A cursor drives every interaction with real clicks and drags, and the camera zooms screen-studio style so each moment fills the frame.\nBrand: {{brand}}. One typeface, one accent color used only on the through-line object and the key word, lots of empty space. Never show a raw screenshot: break screenshots into components and icons and animate those. Juicy motion, like a real production, not a demo.\nMotion rules: springs with at most a small overshoot; nothing appears from nothing (it grows, unfolds or is revealed by a mask); the camera never sits still (push from 1.0 to 1.04 across a scene, and start each scene at the zoom the last one ended on); something changes on every beat.\nBanned: particle bursts, glows, lens flares, camera shake, RGB split, crossfades, generic gradients, bouncy easing, holds longer than half a second, anything that looks like a template.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM. Write a timed storyboard first, beat by beat, then build exactly that.\nHook (first bar): the problem in three to five words, in big type that the through-line object interrupts.\nMiddle: three or four moments of the product doing what it does best. Each begins with the through-line object turning into the next UI element and lands one readable idea.\nPayoff (last bars): the strongest moment on the biggest beat, then the through-line object becomes the logo and the end card: the name and {{ending}}.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a kick on every beat and a hit on each scene change. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<gotchas>\nMeasure element positions at runtime for match cuts instead of hard-coding them. When a shape fills the frame, scale it past all four corners. Give outgoing and incoming text separate timing so letters never overlap. Never put will-change on anything the camera scales, or text renders blurry.\n</gotchas>\n\n<start>\nShow me what you found, the through-line object and a beat-by-beat storyboard, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the final MP4.\n</start>",
