    "summary": "Verified motion prompt by @dale_vaz",
    "contentBody": "create a self contained aesthetically pleasing, breathtaking animation of the Sahi Trading Terminal. it should start with a single dot on a black screen and then have multiple windows show up on the screen such as Charts, Positons , Option Chain, Watchlist. And zoom into and out at multiple scales. Finally the camera zooms out to make the desktop look like a point in space. And then it zooms back in to reveal a mobile screen in landscape mode with a chart in one half and an option chain in another. The video ends in Sjngle Screen Trading on SAHI\\nuse javascript for it. be detailed and accurate. it should be visually stunning",
    "originalPrompt": "create a self contained aesthetically pleasing, breathtaking animation of the Sahi Trading Terminal. it should start with a single dot on a black screen and then have multiple windows show up on the screen such as Charts, Positons , Option Chain, Watchlist. And zoom into and out at multiple scales. Finally the camera zooms out to make the desktop look like a point in space. And then it zooms back in to reveal a mobile screen in landscape mode with a chart in one half and an option chain in another. The video ends in Sjngle Screen Trading on SAHI\\nuse javascript for it. be detailed and accurate. it should be visually stunning",
    "improvedPrompt": "<direction>\nA {{length}}-second, breathtaking animation of {{product}}, in JavaScript. Be detailed and accurate: real-looking data, real layouts.\nThe journey: {{journey}}\nIt ends on \"{{ending}}\".\nBanned: particle bursts, glows, lens flares, camera shake, RGB split, crossfades, generic gradients, bouncy easing, holds longer than half a second, anything that looks like a template.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM. Each window lands on a beat; each zoom spans a bar; the pull-out to a point in space is the strongest moment.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a soft hit as each window lands and a swell on the big zoom. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<start>\nShow me a storyboard with every scale change, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the final MP4.\n</start>",
    "template": "<direction>\nA {{length}}-second, breathtaking animation of {{product}}, in JavaScript. Be detailed and accurate: real-looking data, real layouts.\nThe journey: {{journey}}\nIt ends on \"{{ending}}\".\nBanned: particle bursts, glows, lens flares, camera shake, RGB split, crossfades, generic gradients, bouncy easing, holds longer than half a second, anything that looks like a template.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM. Each window lands on a beat; each zoom spans a bar; the pull-out to a point in space is the strongest moment.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a soft hit as each window lands and a swell on the big zoom. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<start>\nShow me a storyboard with every scale change, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the final MP4.\n</start>",
