    "contentBody": "Render a recipe motion graphic animation using JavaScript or HTML (whatever you think will produce the best result) to show the full recipe for a White Russian from start to finish, empty glass to completed cocktail. Explainer video style, showing the ingredients and measurements as they go into the glass. It should be a 30s video.",
    "originalPrompt": "Render a recipe motion graphic animation using JavaScript or HTML (whatever you think will produce the best result) to show the full recipe for a White Russian from start to finish, empty glass to completed cocktail. Explainer video style, showing the ingredients and measurements as they go into the glass. It should be a 30s video.",
    "improvedPrompt": "<direction>\nA {{length}}-second recipe explainer for {{drink}}, from an empty glass to the finished drink.\nShow each ingredient and its measurement as it goes into the glass: the pour, the level rising, the measure in clean type beside it. Then the final touches ({{finish}}) and a moment to admire it.\nStyle: {{style}}.\nBanned: stock clip art, glows, generic gradients, measurements you'd have to pause to read.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM. One ingredient per bar, the glass in the same spot throughout, the camera easing in for the finish.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a pour, ice or clink sound on each step, under light music. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<start>\nList the recipe with measurements, then show a storyboard, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the final MP4.\n</start>",
    "template": "<direction>\nA {{length}}-second recipe explainer for {{drink}}, from an empty glass to the finished drink.\nShow each ingredient and its measurement as it goes into the glass: the pour, the level rising, the measure in clean type beside it. Then the final touches ({{finish}}) and a moment to admire it.\nStyle: {{style}}.\nBanned: stock clip art, glows, generic gradients, measurements you'd have to pause to read.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM. One ingredient per bar, the glass in the same spot throughout, the camera easing in for the finish.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a pour, ice or clink sound on each step, under light music. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<start>\nList the recipe with measurements, then show a storyboard, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the final MP4.\n</start>",
