    "contentBody": "make a dynamic 20-second motion graphics and animation video that shows what an incredible motion designer and animator you are, like it's your showreel for a résumé. make it about the cycle of life showing the same individual going from childhood to adolescence to the 9-5 cycle, to family life to elder life and death, then cutting ready to loop to the beginning.  go all out , use whatever you need.",
    "originalPrompt": "make a dynamic 20-second motion graphics and animation video that shows what an incredible motion designer and animator you are, like it's your showreel for a résumé. make it about the cycle of life showing the same individual going from childhood to adolescence to the 9-5 cycle, to family life to elder life and death, then cutting ready to loop to the beginning.  go all out , use whatever you need.",
    "improvedPrompt": "<direction>\nA {{length}}-second motion design showreel that proves you are a world-class motion designer, built around {{theme}}. Pick one visual concept from it and commit to it: name it in one line before you start. Every shot is made out of the previous one: shapes, type and color transform into the next scene instead of cutting or fading.\nOne typeface, a palette of {{palette}}, one consistent camera language.\nIt's character animation as much as motion design.\nShow range: kinetic type, shape morphing, a 2D-to-3D moment, a data or UI moment, and an end title. Give each one an equal share of the time.\nBanned: particle bursts, glows, lens flares, camera shake, RGB split, crossfades, generic gradients, bouncy easing, holds longer than half a second, anything that looks like a template.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM, something happens on every beat.\nFirst bar: hook. Then one technique per bar, each flowing out of the last. Last full bar: the strongest moment. Final half bar: end title, and the final frame matches the first so it loops.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a kick on every beat, a riser into the last full bar, a hit on each scene change. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<start>\nTell me the concept and show a beat-by-beat storyboard, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the final MP4.\n</start>",
    "template": "<direction>\nA {{length}}-second motion design showreel that proves you are a world-class motion designer, built around {{theme}}. Pick one visual concept from it and commit to it: name it in one line before you start. Every shot is made out of the previous one: shapes, type and color transform into the next scene instead of cutting or fading.\nOne typeface, a palette of {{palette}}, one consistent camera language.\nIt's character animation as much as motion design.\nShow range: kinetic type, shape morphing, a 2D-to-3D moment, a data or UI moment, and an end title. Give each one an equal share of the time.\nBanned: particle bursts, glows, lens flares, camera shake, RGB split, crossfades, generic gradients, bouncy easing, holds longer than half a second, anything that looks like a template.\n</direction>\n\n<structure>\n{{bpm}} BPM, something happens on every beat.\nFirst bar: hook. Then one technique per bar, each flowing out of the last. Last full bar: the strongest moment. Final half bar: end title, and the final frame matches the first so it loops.\n</structure>\n\n<build>\n1. One HTML file at {{size}}. Every style is computed from time inside window.seek(t): no CSS animations, no timers, no state carried between frames.\n2. Use spring and exponential easing written as functions of time. Blur follows speed and direction on fast moves.\n3. Write a soundtrack in code with the Web Audio API (OfflineAudioContext): a kick on every beat, a riser into the last full bar, a hit on each scene change. Render it to WAV.\n4. Render with Playwright at 60fps, 3 subframes per frame blended with ffmpeg tmix for motion blur, then mux the audio.\n5. Before the full render, render one frame per beat and look at them. Fix anything cluttered, off the beat, overlapping or hard to read.\n</build>\n\n<start>\nTell me the concept and show a beat-by-beat storyboard, then build it without asking me anything else. Give me the final MP4.\n</start>",
